.TH flashalign 1 "26 September 2026" "flashalign-0.1.0" "Bioinformatics tools"
.SH NAME
.PP
flashalign \- long-read DNA and spliced RNA alignment against a reference genome
.SH SYNOPSIS
* Indexing the reference (optional):
.RS 4
flashalign index
.RB [ \-x
.IR preset ]
.I ref.fa
.RI [ out.faix ]
.br
flashalign index
.RB [ \-k
.IR kmer ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR smer ]
.RB [ \-I
.IR batchSize ]
.I ref.fa
.RI [ out.faix ]
.RE

* Long-read alignment with CIGAR:
.RS 4
flashalign align
.B \-a
.RB [ \-x
.IR preset ]
.I ref.faix
.I reads.fq
>
.I output.sam
.br
flashalign align
.B \-c
.RB [ \-x
.IR preset ]
.I ref.fa
.I reads.fq
>
.I output.paf
.RE

* Long-read mapping without CIGAR:
.RS 4
flashalign align
.RB [ \-x
.IR preset ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR nThreads ]
.I ref.fa
.I reads.fq
>
.I output.paf
.RE

* Spliced alignment of long RNA reads:
.RS 4
flashalign align
.B \-ax splice
.I ref.fa
.I reads.fq
>
.I output.sam
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
FlashAlign maps long reads to a reference genome: noisy Nanopore reads, accurate
long reads such as PacBio HiFi, and long cDNA, direct-RNA and Iso-Seq reads,
which it aligns across introns. It outputs in the PAF or SAM format, PAF by
default; it writes no BAM, so BAM is made by piping SAM to
.BR samtools .
.PP
Seeds are closed syncmers, so an index is defined by the pair
.RI ( k ,\  s ).
.B flashalign index
writes a reusable
.I .faix
index
.RI ( ref.fa .faix
unless
.I out.faix
is given) and records in it the preset it was built with;
.B flashalign align
adopts that preset unless
.B \-x
is given. The target of
.B align
may equally be a reference FASTA, which is indexed transiently in memory and
never written out. FASTA or FASTQ (plain or gzip) and
.I .faix
targets are told apart by content, not by name, and any other file is refused.
.PP
The index owns the seeding:
.B align
has no
.B \-k
and no
.BR \-s .
A FASTA target is indexed with the preset's
.RI ( k ,\  s ),
a
.I .faix
target supplies its own, and mapping under another pair means running
.B flashalign index
.RB \-k / \-s
first.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Indexing options
.TP 10
.B \-x, \-\-preset \fISTR\fR
Preset [lr]. It fixes the seeding of the index and is written into it. Available
.I STR
are
.B lr
.RB ( \-k21
.BR \-s9 ),
.B lr:hq
.RB ( \-k21
.BR \-s5 ),
.B splice
and
.B splice:hq
.RB ( \-k15
.BR \-s10 ),
the experimental
.BR asm5 ,
.B asm10
and
.B asm20
.RB ( \-k21
.BR \-s9 ),
and the experimental
.B ava\-ont
.RB ( \-k17
.BR \-s9 )
and
.B ava\-hifi
.RB ( \-k21
.BR \-s5 ).
See
.BR "Preset options" .
.TP
.BI \-k \ INT
Syncmer k-mer length [21], no larger than 23. Every preset but the experimental
assembly and overlap ones maps an index of any such k; Nanopore direct RNA maps on a
.B \-k14
splice index. A k-mer that is its own reverse complement,
possible at an even value only, is never a seed.
.TP
.BI \-s \ INT
Closed-syncmer s-mer size, 1 to
.I k
[9]. A closed syncmer is a k-mer whose minimal s-mer sits at one of its two
ends; the seed density is about 2/(k\-s+1). A preset's s larger than
.I k
is taken as
.IR k .
.TP
.BI \-I \ NUM
Load at most
.I NUM
target bases into RAM for indexing [no split]. If there are more than
.I NUM
bases in
.IR ref.fa ,
flashalign needs to read
.I reads.fq
multiple times to map it against each batch of target sequences. This would
create a multi-part index.
.I NUM
may be ending with k/K/m/M/g/G. NB: mapping quality is incorrect given a
multi-part index.
.TP
.B \-\-idx\-no\-seq
Don't store target sequences in the index. It saves disk space and memory but
the index generated with this option will not work with
.B \-a
or
.BR \-c :
it serves plain PAF output only, so SAM output,
.BR \-\-cs ,
.B \-\-MD
and the splice presets refuse it. This option is not listed on the help screen.
.TP
.BI \-t \ INT
Number of threads [all].
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Suppress non-error logging, including the version, command line and resource
usage printed at the end of the run.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the
.B index
help message to stdout and exit.
.SS Placement options
.TP 10
.BI \-g \ NUM
Stop alignment elongation if there are no seeds within
.IR NUM -bp
[5000]. It bounds how far base alignment extends toward a read end, and under a
DNA preset two pieces of a read join into one record only across gaps of at most
.IR NUM .
Under a DNA preset it is also the maximum gap of chaining, whose reach is never
below the chain's band or a quarter of the read length (up to 131072), so values
under the band leave that chain unchanged. The band is
.I NUM2
of
.B \-r
for the dense chains and
.B \-\-screen\-band
for the screening chain, both 20000 under
.B lr
and
.BR lr:hq .
Under a splice preset it bounds base alignment and is also the maximum
query gap of the fine chain, which is 20000 under
.B splice
and 10000 under
.B splice:hq
when
.B \-g
is not given; there
.B \-G
bounds introns.
.B lr
uses 5000,
.B lr:hq
10000 and the splice presets 2000.
.I NUM
may be ending with k/K/m/M/g/G.
.TP
.B \-r \fINUM1\fR[,\fINUM2\fR]
Bandwidth for base alignment and chaining [500,20000].
Under a DNA preset,
.I NUM1
is used for alignment extension toward the read ends and for inverted middles,
only when base-level alignment runs
.RB ( \-c ,
SAM).
.I NUM2
is used for closing gaps between anchors and as the diagonal band of the dense
chains, so it also acts on placement and plain PAF output; the screening chain
keeps its own band
.RB ( \-\-screen\-band ).
A single value sets
.I NUM1
only. Each value may be ending with k/K/m/M/g/G, and
.I NUM1
may not exceed
.IR NUM2 .
Under a splice preset
.I NUM1
is the diagonal band of the fine chain and
.I NUM2
only bounds
.IR NUM1 ;
neither bands base alignment there, and
.B \-G
sets both [200000,200000].
.TP
.BI \-m \ INT
Discard alignments with chaining score
.RI < INT
[40; 100 on ava\-ont]. Chaining score equals the approximate number of matching bases minus a
concave gap penalty. With a CIGAR the test counts the alignment's matching
bases; in plain PAF output it reads the chain score. A rival chain scoring under
.I INT
is weighed as scoring
.I INT
in the mapping quality; an inverted middle is aligned only when both its spans
reach
.IR INT ;
and base alignment trims a bad read end until
.RI 2* INT
matching bases. At least 1. The long form is
.BR \-\-min\-chain\-score .
DNA presets only.
.TP
.BI \-p \ FLOAT
Minimal secondary-to-primary score ratio to output secondary mappings [0.8].
Under a splice preset it
retains a rival: a retained rival is weighed in the mapping quality, is what
.BR s2 ,
.B md
and
.B XA
report, and is a record of its own under
.BR \-\-secondary=yes .
Under a DNA preset it decides which alternative loci enter the ownership
selection, which chains attached to an owner are aligned beside it and kept as
secondary alignments, and which rival a piece of a split read is weighed against
in the mapping quality: one whose chain scores at least
.I FLOAT
times the primary's, or within 2\fIk\fR of it.
.TP
.BI \-N \ INT
Output at most
.I INT
secondary alignments [5; DNA presets 1]. They are printed as secondary records
under
.BR \-\-secondary=yes .
On the splice presets the best locus and at most
.I INT
rivals are kept, which
.B s2:i
and the mapping quality weigh; with a CIGAR, up to
.I INT
of them are realized for
.B XA:Z
and
.BR md:i .
On the DNA presets, with
.BR \-\-secondary=yes ,
the chains of up to
.I INT
alternative loci enter the ownership selection (without it, of one); with a
CIGAR at most 5 secondary alignments are kept per read, a number
.B \-N
does not set.
.B \-N0
turns secondary output off, as
.BR \-\-secondary=no .
.TP
.BI \-\-rival\-min\-diff \ INT
Absolute chain-score band that retains a rival beside
.B \-p
[30]. Splice presets only.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-support \ INT
Minimal number of seeds on a vote peak [3]. The splice presets use 2.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-vote\-occ \ INT
Ignore a seed whose reference occurrence is above
.I INT
when voting, and under a DNA preset when chaining too; 0 turns occurrence
filtering off [200]. Unset, the cap is the larger of 200 and the occurrence of the
top 1.81e\-4 fraction of the most frequent seeds in the index, at most 500 under
.BR lr:hq ,
and the DNA chaining gates take the same cap. The splice presets keep the fixed
200. Under a DNA preset a vote tile whose seeds are all above the cap still votes
with its rarest seed of at most 4095 occurrences
.RB ( lr )
or 1024
.RB ( lr:hq ),
and chaining keeps that seed.
.TP
.BI \-\-vote\-ratio \ FLOAT
Keep every vote peak whose vote is at least
.I FLOAT
of the best peak's [0.25; 0 on ava\-ont, ava\-hifi]. 0 keeps the top four peaks by
count instead. DNA presets only.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-chain\-occ \ INT
Drop a seed whose genome-wide reference occurrence is above
.I INT
from the pool handed to chaining; 0 disables the gate [200]. Unset, the gate is
the voting cap
.RB ( \-\-max\-vote\-occ ).
A seed an empty vote tile kept passes the gate. Only this gate bounds the
seeds chaining receives: with 0, repeats can cost several times the CPU and
memory. DNA presets only.
.TP
.BI \-\-dw \ INT
Width in reference bases of the diagonal bin a seed votes into, greater than 0
[64].
.B lr:hq
and
.B splice:hq
use 48. Under a DNA preset the width otherwise grows with the read length; a
value given here fixes it and turns that growth off.
.TP
.BI \-\-dw\-slope\-den \ INT
Denominator of the term that grows the vote bin with read length: the width is
the read length divided by
.IR INT ,
held between the base width and the cap [128].
.BR lr:hq ,
.B ava\-ont
and
.B ava\-hifi
use 64, and 0 drops the term. DNA presets only.
.TP
.BI \-\-dw\-max \ INT
Largest width that growth may reach, greater than 0 [2048]. While the growth is
on it may not be below the width
.B \-\-dw
sets. DNA presets only.
.TP
.BI \-\-vote\-seeds \ INT
Seeds per strand that vote [128; 4096 on asm5, asm10, asm20; 256 on ava\-ont]: the read is cut
into
.I INT
equal tiles and each tile votes with one of its seeds; 0 votes with every seed,
and every preset takes the value. Above 128, a DNA preset keeps the seeds the
128\-tile cut picks and adds seeds of the
.IR INT \-tile
cut, each the farthest from those kept, until it holds as many as that cut picks;
a rescued seed that would exceed the strand's budget of rescued occurrences is
skipped, so the count can stop short. The exact refinement of the vote peaks uses the seeds of both
cuts. Neither
.B \-\-min\-support
nor
.B \-\-max\-cands
scales with
.IR INT ;
a vote peak needs
.B \-\-min\-support
votes (3 on the DNA presets), so an
.I INT
below that places few reads or none.
The Python field is
.BR Config.max_query_seeds .
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-cands \ INT
Vote peaks and placement candidates kept per strand, 1 to 64 [16]; 1 to 16383
[16383] under
.B ava\-ont
and
.BR ava\-hifi ,
which partition nothing. Elsewhere, unset, it is
16 when vote peaks are admitted by their ratio to the best
.RB ( \-\-vote\-ratio ),
where it only bounds the cost, and 4 when
.B \-\-vote\-ratio
is 0, where it selects; a value replaces whichever applies. The
.B \-N
ranks are drawn from these candidates, so a wider bound can add secondaries.
DNA presets only.
.TP
.BI \-\-tiles \ INT
Query tiles the read is cut into for placement, 2 to 4096 [128; 2048 on asm5,
asm10, asm20]. The exact query
partition gives each tile one candidate or none, and an owned block needs two
tiles with seed support. Under the default
.BR \-\-tile\-score ,
2 tiles place no read: a two\-tile block scores 8 \- 12 = \-4, below the \-2
of leaving both tiles unowned. The
.B \-\-tile\-score
terms are per tile, so their weight against each other and against the read's
length moves with
.IR INT .
A candidate starts with the tiles in which a voting seed supports it, so with
fewer voting seeds than tiles
.RB ( \-\-vote\-seeds )
most tiles start unsupported and candidates are hard to admit; keep
.B \-\-vote\-seeds
at least
.IR INT .
Above 2048 tiles, candidates are admitted by comparing their supports on 2048
tiles. The partition's work grows with
.IR INT .
DNA presets only; the splice presets keep 128.
.TP
.B \-\-tile\-score \fIhit\fR=\fIINT\fR,\fIblock\fR=\fIINT\fR,\fInull\fR=\fIINT\fR,\fImiss\fR=\fIINT\fR
Objective of the exact query partition, which decides for each of the read's
query tiles
.RB ( \-\-tiles )
which candidate owns it [hit=4,block=12,null=1,miss=0]. Under a DNA preset the
partition picks the candidates chained over the whole read, and the anchors of
those chains then decide which chain owns each part of the read.
.I hit
rewards an owned tile the seeds support and is at least 1;
.I block
is charged each time an ownership block opens,
.I null
per valid tile left unowned, and
.I miss
per owned tile without seed support, each at least 0. Any subset of the keys may
be given, and a repeated key keeps its last value. DNA presets only.
.TP
.BI \-\-screen\-band \ NUM
Diagonal band of placement's bounded screening chain [20000]. It keeps the
anchors whose diagonal lies within
.I NUM
of the candidate's and chains them at a bandwidth of
.I NUM
or a quarter of the read length (up to 131072), whichever is larger. The dense
chains band at
.I NUM2
of
.BR \-r .
.I NUM
is at least 1 and may be ending with k/K/m/M/g/G. DNA presets only;
.B ava\-ont
and
.BR ava\-hifi ,
which run no screening pass, refuse it.
.SS Alignment options
Under a DNA preset,
.BR \-A ", " \-B ", " \-O ", " \-E ", " \-z " and " \-\-score\-N
set the scoring of the gap fills between anchors, and that row is checked:
.B \-O
and
.B \-E
positive, E1>E2 and O1+E1<O2+E2 unless the two pieces are equal,
(O1+E1)+(O2+E2) at most 127,
.B \-\-score\-N
below
.BR \-B ,
.B \-A
and
.B \-B
within [1,127] and
.B \-\-end\-bonus
at least \-1; a row that fails exits 1. The read-end extensions use
the preset's own row, which also scores every alignment, so AS,
.B \-S
and
.B \-\-end\-bonus
are in its units: for
.B lr
.BR \-A2 " " \-B4 " " \-O4,24 " " \-E2,1 " " \-z400,200 " " \-\-score\-N " 1,"
for
.B lr:hq
.BR \-A1 " " \-B4 " " \-O6,26 " " \-E2,1 " " \-z400,200 " " \-\-score\-N " 1."
Under
.BR asm5 ,
.B asm10
and
.BR asm20 ,
as under the splice presets, the gap fills and the read-end extensions use one
row, and these options set it.
The defaults below are those of
.BR lr .
.TP 10
.BI \-A \ INT
Matching score [4].
.TP
.BI \-B \ INT
Mismatching penalty [8]. Use a larger value for lower divergence.
.TP
.B \-O \fIINT\fR[,\fIINT\fR]
Gap open penalty [8,48]. If the second
.I INT
is not specified, it is set to the first.
.TP
.B \-E \fIINT\fR[,\fIINT\fR]
Gap extension penalty [4,1]. A gap of length
.I k
costs
.RI min{ O1 + k * E1 , O2 + k * E2 }.
If the second
.I INT
is not specified, it is set to the first.
.TP
.BI \-z \ INT1[,INT2]
Truncate an alignment if the running alignment score drops too quickly along the
diagonal of the DP matrix (diagonal X-drop, or Z-drop) [800,200]. Under a DNA
preset, a drop above
.I INT2
tests the dropped region for a small inversion, aligning the reverse complement
of the query there; under
.BR lr:hq ,
only where the read's own seeds of the other strand chain inside that region.
If
.I INT2
is not specified, it is set to
.IR INT1 ;
it may not exceed
.IR INT1 .
.TP
.BI \-S \ INT
Minimal peak DP alignment score to output [80]. Under a DNA preset an
alignment also needs at least
.B \-m
(40) matching bases; a value of 0 or less turns off the score test, not the base
count. Plain PAF output, which has no DP, is not filtered by this score; under a
DNA preset it prints no record whose chaining score is below
.BR \-m .
The long form is
.BR \-\-min\-dp\-score .
.TP
.BI \-\-score\-N \ INT
Penalty of a mismatch involving ambiguous bases [2].
.TP
.BI \-\-end\-bonus \ INT
Score bonus when alignment extends to the end of the query sequence [\-1].
.SS Splice options
.TP 10
.BI \-\-min\-intron \ INT
Minimal reference gap that chaining accepts as an intron [20]; a shorter gap is
left to base alignment as a deletion. It does not decide the `N' CIGAR operator:
base alignment writes a gap as `N' once the second gap penalty makes the long
gap cheaper (see
.B \-O
and
.BR \-E ).
Splice presets only.
.TP
.BI \-G \ NUM
Maximum gap on the reference (effective with a splice preset), that is the
maximum intron length [200k]. It also sets both values of
.BR \-r ,
as in minimap2; when both
.B \-G
and
.B \-r
are given, the later one decides them.
.I NUM
may be ending with k/K/m/M/g/G. The long form is
.BR \-\-max\-intron\-len .
Splice presets only.
.TP
.BI \-u \ CHAR
How to find canonical splicing sites GT-AG \-
.BR f :
transcript strand;
.BR b :
both strands;
.BR r :
reverse strand;
.BR n :
no attempt to match GT-AG [b]. Under
.B n
no
.B ts
tag is stamped. Use
.B f
for reads already on the transcript strand, such as direct RNA and Iso-Seq.
Splice presets only.
.TP
.BI \-\-junc\-bed \ FILE
Junctions to prefer during base alignment [].
.I FILE
can be gene annotations in the BED12 format (aka 12-column BED), or intron
positions in 6-column BED with the strand column required. BED12 file can be
converted from GTF/GFF3 with `paftools.js gff2bed anno.gtf'. Splice presets
only.
.TP
.BI \-\-junc\-bonus \ INT
Score bonus for a splice donor or acceptor found in annotation [9]. Effective
with
.BR \-\-junc\-bed .
.I INT
ranges from 0 to 127. Splice presets only.
.SS Input/output options
.TP 10
.B \-o, \-\-output \fIFILE\fR
Output alignments to
.I FILE
[stdout].
.TP
.B \-c
Generate CIGAR. In PAF, the CIGAR is written to the `cg' custom tag. PAF
without
.B \-c
is placement-only: it runs no base-level dynamic programming, so its
coordinates and its
.B AS:i
differ from those of a base-level alignment.
.BR \-c ,
.B \-\-cs
and
.B \-\-MD
turn base-level alignment on in PAF; SAM output always has it.
.TP
.BR \-\-cs [= short | long ]
Output the
.B cs
tag. If no argument is given,
.RB ` short '
is set [none]. Implies
.BR \-c .
.TP
.B \-\-MD
Output the MD tag (see the SAM spec). Implies
.BR \-c .
.TP
.B \-\-eqx
Output =/X CIGAR operators for sequence match/mismatch. It rewrites every CIGAR
the run realizes \- the SAM CIGAR,
.B SA:Z
and PAF's
.B cg:Z
\- and implies nothing, so plain PAF needs
.B \-c
beside it.
.TP
.B \-Y, \-\-soft\-clip\-supp
In SAM output, use soft clipping for supplementary alignments. The default is
hard clipping.
.TP
.B \-R, \-\-rg \fISTR\fR
SAM read group line in a format like
.B @RG\\\\tID:foo\\\\tSM:bar
[]. It must start with
.B @RG
and carry an
.B ID
field, and it adds
.B RG:Z
as the first tag of every SAM record.
.TP
.B \-\-sam\-hit\-only
In SAM, don't output unmapped reads.
.TP
.B \-\-paf\-no\-hit
In PAF, output unmapped queries; the strand and the reference name fields are
set to `*', and every count to 0.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-header
Don't output the SAM header.
.TP
.BR \-\-dual = yes | no
With an overlap preset, whether an overlap is printed from each of its reads
that finds it
.RB ( yes )
or once, from the read whose name sorts first
.RB ( no ),
as minimap2's
.B \-\-dual
[no]. With
.B no
a read is not compared with a target read whose name sorts before its own.
Refused with any other preset.
.TP
.BR \-\-secondary = yes | no
Whether to output secondary alignments [no]. With
.BR yes ,
each alternative alignment of a read is written after the primary and its
supplementary records: FLAG 0x100, or 0x900 on its continuation segments,
.B tp:A:S
in PAF, mapping quality 0, and
.RB ` * '
for SEQ and QUAL. A secondary record carries
.B ms
and its own chain's
.B cm
and
.BR s1 ,
but no
.BR md ,
.BR s2 ,
.B SA
or
.BR XA .
Under a DNA preset the secondary alignments are those of the chains aligned
beside an owner that, after alignment, overlap a primary record by more than
half of the shorter, at most 5 per read; they are listed on the primary's
.BR XA .
Without a CIGAR a DNA preset writes no secondary alignment.
The value may also be given as a separate word
.RB ( "\-\-secondary yes" );
typed twice, the last one wins.
.TP
.B \-y
Copy input FASTA/Q comments to output, verbatim, as the last field of every
record the read produces.
.TP
.BI \-t \ INT
Number of threads [all\-2]: mapping runs on the machine's hardware threads less
the reader and the writer. An explicit value is the whole mapping budget, and
decompression of compressed input borrows from it rather than adding to it.
.TP
.B \-K, \-\-batch\-bp \fINUM\fR
Number of bases loaded into memory to process in a mini-batch [500M]. As with
.BR \-I ,
a k/K/m/M/g/G suffix is accepted. Output bytes and output order do not depend on
it.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-config
Print the fully resolved configuration to stdout and exit 0. It loads neither
the reference nor the reads, so
.B flashalign align \-x lr:hq \-\-show\-config
is a complete invocation; of a
.I .faix
target it reads the header alone.
.TP
.B \-\-stats
Print a summary of the run to stderr: reads and bases, mapped reads, records
written, wall time, throughput and peak memory.
.TP
.B \-\-progress
Print per-batch progress to stderr.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Suppress non-error logging, including the three lines a run ends with on stderr:
the version, the command line, and the real time, CPU time and peak memory.
.TP
.B \-a
Generate CIGAR and output alignments in the SAM format. FlashAlign outputs in
PAF by default.
.TP
.BI \-\-batch\-window \ INT
Number of batches in flight in the compute stage [3]. With 1, all threads finish
a batch before the next one starts. Output bytes and order are identical for
every value.
.TP
.BI \-\-io\-staging \ MIB
Ceiling, in MiB, on the decompressed input staged ahead of the reader; 0 scales
it with
.B \-t
[0]. A non-zero value must be at least 64.
.SS Preset options
.TP 10
.B \-x, \-\-preset \fISTR\fR
Preset [lr]. This option applies multiple options at the same time. An explicit
.B \-x
also overrides the preset recorded in a
.I .faix
target; the index keeps its own
.RI ( k ", " s ).
Available
.I STR
are:
.RS
.TP 10
.B lr
Align noisy long reads of ~10% error rate to a reference genome. This is the
default mode.
.RB ( \-k21
.B \-s9 \-\-min\-support 3 \-A4 \-B8 \-O8,48 \-E4,1
.B \-z800,200 \-\-score\-N 2 \-r500,20000 \-g5000 \-m40 \-p0.8
.BR \-S80 ).
.TP
.B lr:hq
Align accurate long reads (error rate <1%), such as PacBio HiFi reads, to a
reference genome
.RB ( \-k21
.B \-s5 \-\-min\-support 3 \-A3 \-B12 \-O18,78 \-E6,1
.B \-z1200,600 \-\-score\-N 3 \-r500,20000 \-g10000 \-m40 \-p0.8
.BR \-S200 ).
Its alignments are scored with minimap2's map-hifi scoring
.RB ( \-A1 " " \-B4 " " \-O6,26 " " \-E2,1 ,
.BR \-S200 ).
.TP
.B splice
Long-read spliced alignment
.RB ( \-k15
.B \-s10 \-\-min\-support 2 \-A1 \-B2 \-O2,32 \-E1,0 \-z200 \-S80 \-g2000
.B \-\-min\-intron 20 \-G200k \-\-junc\-bonus 9 \-ub \-p0.8
.BR \-N5 ).
In the splice mode a long deletion is taken as an intron and represented as the
.RB ` N '
CIGAR operator, and
.B \-m
is refused. For Nanopore direct RNA, index with
.B \-k14
.RB ( "flashalign index \-x splice \-k 14 ref.fa" )
and align with
.BR "\-x splice \-u f" .
.TP
.B splice:hq
Spliced alignment for accurate long RNA-seq reads such as PacBio Iso-Seq
.RB ( \-xsplice
.B \-B4
.BR \-O6,24 ).
It differs from
.B splice
in scoring and also in the vote's diagonal bin (48 against 64), the fine
chain's query gap (10000 against 20000; see
.BR \-g )
and the MAPQ coverage threshold (0.70 against 0.55).
.TP
.B asm5
Experimental. Align an assembly to a reference that it differs from by about
0.1%
.RB ( \-k21
.B \-s9 \-\-min\-support 3 \-A1 \-B19 \-O39,81 \-E3,1
.B \-z200,200 \-\-score\-N 1 \-r1000,100000 \-g10000 \-m40 \-p0.8
.BR "\-S200 \-\-vote\-seeds 4096 \-\-tiles 2048" ).
The gap fills and the read-end extensions use the one scoring row, which
.BR \-A ", " \-B ", " \-O ", " \-E ", " \-z " and " \-\-score\-N
set. A
.I .faix
target must have been built with
.B \-k21
.BR \-s9 ;
another is refused.
.TP
.B asm10
Experimental. As
.B asm5
for a divergence of about 1%
.RB ( \-xasm5
.B \-B9 \-O16,41
.BR \-E2,1 ).
.TP
.B asm20
Experimental. As
.B asm5
for a divergence of several percent
.RB ( \-xasm5
.B \-B4 \-O6,26
.BR \-E2,1 ).
.TP
.B ava\-ont
Experimental. All-vs-all overlap of noisy long reads: map a read set against
itself, or against an index of the same reads built with
.B flashalign index \-x ava\-ont
.RB ( \-k17
.B \-s9 \-\-min\-support 3 \-\-vote\-seeds 256 \-\-vote\-ratio 0
.B \-\-dw\-slope\-den 64 \-\-max\-cands 16383
.B \-m100
.BR \-\-dual=no ).
Each read is found in the target by its name and left out of its own vote and
chain, so read names must be unique. Every chain of score
.B \-m
or more is printed as one PAF record with mapping quality 0 and the tags
.BR tp:A:S ", " cm:i " and " s1:i ,
minimap2's overlap record without
.B dv
and
.BR rl .
An overlap is printed once, from the read whose name sorts first
.RB ( \-\-dual ).
The output is map-only PAF:
.BR \-a ", " \-c ", " \-\-cs " and " \-\-MD
are refused, and so is every option that sets base-level alignment, SAM output,
secondary alignments or the query partition. The index decides
.B \-k
and
.BR \-s ,
as under
.BR lr .
.TP
.B ava\-hifi
Experimental. As
.B ava\-ont
for accurate long reads such as PacBio HiFi, on the seeding of
.B lr:hq
.RB ( \-k21
.B \-s5 \-\-vote\-seeds 128 \-\-vote\-ratio 0 \-\-max\-cands 16383
.B \-m40
.BR \-\-dual=no ).
.RE
.IP
The assembly presets are experimental: they are not qualified on intact
chromosomes. The overlap presets are experimental: they were measured on
simulated reads only, and the Python module refuses them.
.SS Miscellaneous options
.TP 10
.B \-\-help
Print the help screen of the subcommand to stdout and exit 0. A subcommand
invoked with no arguments prints the same screen to stderr and exits 2.
The help screen lists the common options; this page lists every option.
.TP
.B version
Print the version number to stdout.
.B flashalign \-\-version
and
.B flashalign \-V
print the same line. Both spellings are top-level only: neither is an
.B align
or
.B index
option.
.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
.PP
FlashAlign outputs in the PAF format by default; without
.BR \-c ,
PAF output is approximate mapping, with no base-level alignment.
.B \-a
selects the SAM format. FlashAlign writes no BAM; pipe its SAM to
.B samtools sort
for one, as in
.BR "flashalign align \-a ref.fa reads.fq | samtools sort \-o out.bam" .
PAF is a TAB-delimited text format with each line consisting of
at least 12 fields as are described in the following table:
.TS
center box;
cb | cb | cb
r | c | l .
Col	Type	Description
_
1	string	Read name
2	int	Read length
3	int	Read start coordinate (0-based)
4	int	Read end coordinate (0-based)
5	char	`+' for forward strand; `-' for reverse
6	string	Contig name
7	int	Contig length
8	int	Contig start coordinate on the original strand
9	int	Contig end coordinate on the original strand
10	int	Number of matching bases
11	int	Number of bases in alignment
12	int	Mapping quality (0-60)
.TE

.PP
When alignment is available, column 11 gives the total number of sequence
matches, mismatches and gaps in the alignment; column 10 divided by column 11
gives the BLAST-like alignment identity. When alignment is unavailable, these
two columns are approximate. SAM and PAF may optionally have additional
fields in the SAM-like typed key-value format. FlashAlign may output the
following tags:
.TS
center box;
cb | cb | cb
r | c | l .
Tag	Type	Description
_
AS	i	Alignment score from dynamic programming
ms	i	Score of the maximum scoring segment
md	i	Max score minus secondary max score
NM	i	Edit distance
nn	i	Number of ambiguous bases in the alignment
tp	A	Type of aln: P/primary, S/secondary, I/inversion
cm	i	Number of anchors on the chain; FlashAlign's anchors are syncmers
s1	i	Chaining score
s2	i	Chaining score of the best secondary chain
de	f	Gap-compressed per-base sequence divergence
ts	A	Transcript strand (splice mode only)
RG	Z	Read group ID from \-R (SAM only)
SA	Z	List of other supplementary alignments (SAM only)
XA	Z	Secondary hits in (ctg,strandPos,CIGAR,NM;)+ (SAM only)
cg	Z	CIGAR (PAF only)
cs	Z	Difference string
MD	Z	Difference string in the SAM spec
.TE

.PP
Tags
.BR md ,
.B s2
and
.B XA
are not written on a secondary alignment.
.B cm
and
.B s1
describe the chain a record comes from, the chain
.B s2
is compared against; a secondary alignment carries its own chain's. Under the
DNA presets but ava\-ont and ava\-hifi, a primary or supplementary record
carries the anchor count and score of its own part of that chain. A record
without a chain, such as the inverted middle of a DNA alignment, carries none
of the three.
.PP
The
.B cs
tag encodes difference sequences in the short form or the entire query
.I AND
reference sequences in the long form. It consists of a series of operations:
.TS
center box;
cb | cb |cb
r | l | l .
Op	Regex	Description
_
 =	[ACGTN]+	Identical sequence (long form)
 :	[0-9]+	Identical sequence length
 *	[acgtn][acgtn]	Substitution: ref to query
 +	[acgtn]+	Insertion to the reference
 \-	[acgtn]+	Deletion from the reference
 ~	[acgtn]{2}[0-9]+[acgtn]{2}	Intron length and splice signal
.TE

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP 4
.B 0
Success, and every
.B \-\-help
path, which prints to stdout.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime failure: a missing or unreadable input, a write failure, CRAM or SAM
text handed in as reads, a closed output, or an option the resolved preset
cannot honour, such as a scoring row that fails its check or a
.B \-r
pair whose first value exceeds the second. One error line on stderr, no hint.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: an unknown command, an unknown option, a bad value or a missing
operand. One error line plus one hint line on stderr.
.B flashalign
with no arguments, and
.B flashalign align
or
.B flashalign index
with no arguments, print their help screen to stderr and exit 2 instead.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.TP 2
*
Long reads only: a read has to carry enough anchors to be collapsed into one or
more diagonals.
.TP
*
FlashAlign requires SSE4.1 instructions on x86 CPUs or NEON on ARM CPUs. A build
without them is not provided.
.TP
*
The assembly presets
.BR asm5 ,
.B asm10
and
.B asm20
are experimental: they are not qualified on intact chromosomes.
.TP
*
The overlap presets
.B ava\-ont
and
.B ava\-hifi
are experimental: they were measured on simulated reads only, and on Nanopore
reads find fewer overlaps than minimap2's
.BR "\-x ava\-ont" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
samtools(1).
